You can create a button that copies a link to the clipboard in React by using the Clipboard API. Here's an example:
```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

function CopyLinkButton({ link }) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function copyToClipboard() {
    inputRef.current.select();
    document.execCommand('copy');
  }

  return (
    <>
      <input type="text" value={link} ref={inputRef} readOnly />
      <button onClick={copyToClipboard}>Copy Link</button>
    </>
  );
}

export default CopyLinkButton;
```
In this example, we create a component called `CopyLinkButton` that takes a `link` prop. We also create a `useRef` hook to create a reference to the `input` element that will contain the link, and a `copyToClipboard` function that will be called when the button is clicked.

The `copyToClipboard` function first selects the contents of the input element using the `select` method of the `inputRef` reference. It then calls the `execCommand('copy')` method on the `document` object to copy the selected text to the clipboard.

Finally, we render an `input` element with the `link` prop as its value, and pass the `inputRef` reference to the `ref` prop. We also render a `button` element with an `onClick` handler that calls the `copyToClipboard` function.

When the user clicks the button, the link will be copied to their clipboard.

Here is an example of a GraphQL mutation to read a CSV file using `pothos-graphql` and `fastify`:
```
mutation readCSVFile($file: Upload!) {
  readCSV(file: $file) {
    rows {
      cells
    }
  }
}
```
The `readCSV` function would be implemented in your GraphQL resolvers and would use the `fastify` `fileUploader` plugin to process the uploaded file. Here is an example of how the `readCSV` resolver function might be implemented:
```
const readCSV = (obj, args, context, info) => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const { file } = args;

    context.fileUploader.process(file, (err, data) => {
      if (err) {
        return reject(err);
      }

      const rows = data.rows.map(row => ({ cells: row.cells }));
      return resolve({ rows });
    });
  });
};
```
This function takes the uploaded file and uses the `fileUploader` to process it, returning the rows of the CSV file in the expected format for the GraphQL response.

