To make an HTTP request in JavaScript, you can use the built-in `fetch()` function or create an instance of the `XMLHttpRequest` object. Here's an example of how to use `fetch()`:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com/data.json')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error(error);
  });
```
This code makes an HTTP GET request to the URL `https://example.com/data.json`. The response is first checked to see if it is JSON, and then it is logged to the console. If an error occurs, it is caught and logged to the console as well.

Here's an example of how to use `XMLHttpRequest`:
```javascript
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://example.com/data.json');
xhr.responseType = 'json';
xhr.onload = () => {
  console.log(xhr.response);
};
xhr.onerror = () => {
  console.error('Error!');
};
xhr.send();
```
This code also makes an HTTP GET request to the URL `https://example.com/data.json`, but it uses the `XMLHttpRequest` object instead of `fetch()`. The `responseType` property is set to `'json'` so that the response is automatically parsed as JSON. When the response is received, it is logged to the console. If an error occurs, an error message is logged to the console instead.

